; MM-PAR-6: what a `parallel` binding may CAPTURE
; (docs/memory-model-v2-design.md section 3.2b).
;
; A binding runs BESIDE its parent, and `axiom_retain`/`axiom_release`
; are a plain load-add-store rather than an `atomicrmw`. So a heap
; value a thunk names is a block whose count two threads touch with no
; fence between them, they lose an increment, and the block is freed
; while a live reference still names it. What a binding may share with
; its parent is a WORD.
;
; The rule is unconditional. The checker never sees `--threads`, and a
; diagnostic that appears and disappears with a codegen flag is unlike
; anything else in the AX3xxx band; `__proc_spawn` is exempt because it
; names the ISOLATED lowering itself, which is a statement about that
; primitive rather than about a build flag.
;
; Every row is `;@axiom:effect(io)` because `__par_spawn` carries `IO`:
; a `parallel` is an effect whatever it computes.
;
;   1  `shared` captures a `String` its enclosing function also holds.
;      Two AX3064s, one per BINDING - each binding is its own thunk -
;      and the second occurrence inside `a` draws nothing, which is
;      what pins the once-per-name rule. Each is anchored on the
;      OCCURRENCE and not on the word `parallel`: the occurrence is
;      where the fix is.
;   2  `words` is the control, and the whole point of it: a binding
;      capturing only an `Int` is SILENT. A rule that refused every
;      capture would pass a fixture with row 1 alone.
;   3  `shadowed` binds its own `s` inside the binding. That is not a
;      capture even though an outer `s` of the same spelling is a
;      `String`, and it is what the walk's binder arms exist for.
;   4  `called` reaches a TOP-LEVEL function from inside a binding.
;      Silent: `sink` is a module-level name, and value resolution -
;      which this rule has to agree with - reads the current frame
;      only. Looking below `frameBase` finds every signature the
;      module walk has pushed, and a top-level `(-> String Int)` is an
;      arrow, which classifies as a reference: with that lookup this
;      row is a false AX3064 and so is `tests/stdlib/470-parallel.ax`.
;   5  `container` captures a `Vec`. Silent, and not by accident:
;      `evScalarName` lists `Vec` for the OWNERSHIP reason its own
;      comment gives - a `Vec` takes no share, so there is no count to
;      race over. `tests/stdlib/471-parallel-trap.ax` captures one.
(import IO)

(import Str)

(import Vec)

(:: sink (-> String Int))

(fn (sink s) (strLen s))

(:: shared (-> String Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (shared s)
  (parallel p ((a (+ (strLen s) (strLen s))) (b (strLen s)))
    (+ a b)))

(:: words (-> Int Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (words n)
  (parallel p ((a (* n 2)) (b (+ n 1)))
    (+ a b)))

(:: shadowed (-> String Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (shadowed s)
  (parallel p ((a (let ((s (strConcat "x" "y"))) (strLen s))) (b 2))
    (+ a b)))

(:: called (-> String Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (called s)
  (parallel p ((a (sink "lit")) (b 2))
    (+ a b)))

(:: container (-> (Vec Int) Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (container v)
  (parallel p ((a (vecLen v)) (b 2))
    (+ a b)))

(:: main Int)

(fn (main) 0)
